<!--
    kuna #1288: under a prototype model with extrapop="unknown" the stack
    solver guesses each call's stack-pointer change as 4, the x86-32 return
    address. An x86 16-bit near call stores a two-byte return address, so a
    callee that pops nothing was credited two bytes it never popped and every
    stack slot after the call sat two bytes too high. Here the push after the
    near call landed on the caller's local [bp-2], the local became a third
    argument of the first call, and the final add read the call's AX:

      0x00 caller: push bp; mov bp,sp; sub sp,2; mov word [bp-2],9
                   push 5; push 7; call addtwo; add sp,4
                   push ax; call 0000:farf; add sp,2
                   add ax,[bp-2]; mov sp,bp; pop bp; ret
      0x30 addtwo: returns [bp+4] + [bp+6]
      0x40 farf:   returns [bp+6] + 1 (retf)

    (nasm -f bin, org 0.) The guess's base is now the bytes the call
    instruction itself stores: two for the near call, four for the far one.

    Stage: P6 variables, kuna_calleepop.rs (return_address_bytes), under
    option calleepop. Pass one is option calleepop off, upstream's constant 4;
    pass two is the default.
-->
